{% comment %}
  One customer purchase limit applied to a family of products by handle prefix.

  A prefix row limits every product whose handle starts with the given value to
  the same maximum, each product counted on its own. `late-night-crackers-` with
  a maximum of 4 allows 4 units of `late-night-crackers-ep3` and 4 units of
  `late-night-crackers-ep4`, not 4 across the family. New episodes are limited
  the day they are published, without a configuration change.

  Input:  prefix_handle   the start of an EasyStore product handle. Blank
                          disables the row. Compared in lowercase, because
                          EasyStore product URLs are lowercase even when the
                          administrative value is capitalized.
          prefix_maximum  units per customer, per matching product. 0 disables
                          the row.
          prefix_refresh  timestamp each matching allowance is counted from.
                          Blank falls back to customer_order_limit_refresh_all.

  Liquid cannot ask the store for "every handle starting with X", so the prefix
  is matched against the identifiers the page already carries: the product being
  viewed, the products listed by a collection or a search, and the lines in the
  cart. Each distinct match becomes an ordinary customer-order-limit-row, so a
  prefix limit counts orders and cart quantities exactly like a per-handle one
  and publishes the same rule.

  That covers the surfaces a purchase starts from — product page, collection,
  search, and cart. A matching product added from a section that renders its own
  collection, such as a featured collection on the home page, is not seen while
  it is only on that page; the unit it adds is seen as soon as it reaches the
  cart, where the limit applies again and blocks checkout over the maximum.

  SKUs are matched as well as handles, because an order or cart line does not
  expose the same identifier on every EasyStore store, and a row matches a line
  on either one.

  The inputs are cleared again at the end, for the reason given in
  customer-order-limit-row.liquid: `include` shares the caller's scope on
  EasyStore, so the next row would otherwise inherit these values.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_value = prefix_handle | default: '' | append: '' | strip | downcase %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_maximum = prefix_maximum | default: 0 | plus: 0 %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_refresh = prefix_refresh | default: '' | append: '' | strip %}
{% if customer_order_limit_prefix_value != '' and customer_order_limit_prefix_maximum > 0 %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_size = customer_order_limit_prefix_value | size %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_found = ',' %}

  {% comment %}
    The product being viewed. This is the page a limit has to hold on, so it is
    collected first.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = product.handle %}
  {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = product.sku %}
  {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}

  {% comment %}
    Listing pages, so quick add from a collection or a search result is limited
    by the same rule as the product page.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% for customer_order_limit_prefix_listed in collection.products %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_listed.handle %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_listed.sku %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% for customer_order_limit_prefix_listed in search.results %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_listed.handle %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_listed.sku %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
  {% endfor %}

  {% comment %}
    The cart, so a matching product that reached it from anywhere else is still
    counted and still blocks checkout above the maximum.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% for customer_order_limit_prefix_line in cart.items %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_line.product.handle | default: customer_order_limit_prefix_line.handle %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
    {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_page = customer_order_limit_prefix_line.sku | default: customer_order_limit_prefix_line.variant.sku | default: customer_order_limit_prefix_line.product.sku %}
    {% include 'customer-order-limit-prefix-match', match_value: customer_order_limit_prefix_page %}
  {% endfor %}

  {% comment %}
    Rows are published after every source has been read, rather than as each
    match is found, so a product that appears both on the page and in the cart
    publishes one rule and each rule counts the whole cart.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_handles = customer_order_limit_prefix_found | split: ',' %}
  {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_matched = '' %}
  {% for customer_order_limit_prefix_handle in customer_order_limit_prefix_handles %}
    {% unless customer_order_limit_prefix_handle == '' %}
      {% assign customer_order_limit_prefix_matched = customer_order_limit_prefix_matched | append: ' ' | append: customer_order_limit_prefix_handle %}
      {% include 'customer-order-limit-row', limit_handle: customer_order_limit_prefix_handle, limit_maximum: customer_order_limit_prefix_maximum, limit_refresh: customer_order_limit_prefix_refresh %}
    {% endunless %}
  {% endfor %}

  {% comment %}
    A prefix limit is only as good as what the page could see, so what it saw is
    reported. An empty `matched` on a page showing a product that should be
    limited means the handle does not start with the configured prefix.
  {% endcomment %}
  <script>
    window.customerOrderLimitsV2.prefixes[{{ customer_order_limit_prefix_value | json }}] = {
      maximum: {{ customer_order_limit_prefix_maximum | json }},
      refreshAt: {{ customer_order_limit_prefix_refresh | json }},
      matched: {{ customer_order_limit_prefix_matched | strip | json }}
    };
  </script>
{% endif %}
{% assign prefix_handle = '' %}
{% assign prefix_maximum = 0 %}
{% assign prefix_refresh = '' %}
